WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.280
Containers are packaged application environments that include an application,

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.140
its runtime expectations, libraries,

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.360
and filesystem content needed to run consistently across systems.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.500
They are lighter than traditional virtual machines because they usually share the host

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.320
operating system kernel instead of carrying a full guest operating system.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:23.380
That shared-kernel model is one reason containers are fast and portable,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.080
but it also means the host, runtime,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.420
and container configuration remain important security concerns.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.560
Several terms matter early.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.500
An image is the packaged filesystem and metadata used to create running containers.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.360
A registry stores and distributes images.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:43.020
A runtime starts and manages containers on a host.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:46.600
An orchestrator schedules, connects, scales, updates,

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.860
and supervises many containers across infrastructure.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.680
These layers work together,

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.300
so container security cannot focus on only one file or one running process.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.000
Containers improve deployment consistency,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:04.500
but they do not remove application vulnerabilities, weak dependencies, exposed secrets,

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.880
broad privileges, unsafe network paths, or operational mistakes.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:09.980
In fact,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.160
containers can replicate insecure patterns quickly when an image or deployment template is

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.980
reused at scale.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.840
A small configuration weakness can become a fleet-wide problem if it is built into the

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.220
standard image or pipeline.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:30.040
Container security protects the full lifecycle: source code, build pipeline, image,

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:36.820
registry, runtime configuration, host, network, secrets, orchestration layer, monitoring,

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.480
and response.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.260
The goal is not to slow container adoption.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.040
The goal is to make containerized applications predictable, minimal, observable,

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.980
recoverable, and aligned with the risk decisions the organization believes it is making.
